-- Backfill cities.boundary with real municipal-limit polygons from OpenStreetMap
-- (relation geometries, boundary=administrative), for cities where 017/033/055 never
-- set one and where OSM actually has a town/city-level relation (not just a district).
--
-- Source: OSM Nominatim relation geometries, fetched 2026-08-06. These are OSM
-- community-mapped administrative boundaries, not the official municipal corporation
-- survey limits — good enough as a rental/city-classification starting point per team
-- discussion, but flagged here for anyone who wants to swap in surveyed limits later.
--
-- Coverage: 8 of 10 cities. Angul and Berhampur (Brahmapur) have no town/city-level
-- administrative boundary relation in OSM at all as of this writing (only district-level,
-- which is far too coarse) — left NULL rather than inserting a wrong polygon. Same
-- fallback behaviour as before (nearest-centroid classification) applies to them.
--
-- Jajpur has no municipality relation either; used "Jajpur Sadar" tehsil boundary
-- instead (21.6km x 15.9km) as the closest available proxy — coarser than an actual
-- town limit, refine if this ever causes visibly wrong in-city/outstation classification.


-- Bhubaneswar: OSM relation/7901215 (Bhubaneswar Municipal Corporation, Bhubaneswar (M.Corp.), Khordha, Odisha, India), admin_level=8, ~17.2x15.6km
UPDATE cities
